order-13 Wager exact classification audit (2026-08-14)

Decision: retain exact_finite_decision for all three contracts.

- wager.interpretive_model_checker directly enumerates the complete supplied finite S and E
  carriers and evaluates the definitions of W1, W2, and W3 pointwise.
- wager.finite_model_checker directly enumerates W4 and W5.  Its non-reflexive transitive
  closure test for a dc state on a positive-length step cycle is equivalent to W6 by
  ERIEC.Wager.W6_iff_dc_transGen_self for finite S.
- wager.full_model_checker is the conjunction of those two exact finite decisions over one
  shared decoded FrozenImpl.
- Existing sensitivity fixtures remain unchanged.  Target tests additionally cover a
  positive two-state cycle, an acyclic graph, and a cycle that contains no dc state.

The classification is conditional on duplicate-free complete finite carrier enumerations and
faithful total encodings of all predicates, relations, hinge finsets, and the step relation.

Classification rule shared with order-9

exact_finite_decision requires the Julia checker to independently implement the semantic content
of the corresponding Lean definitions and to decide every quantified case over a supplied complete
finite decoding.  Merely applying opaque caller-supplied predicates, repackaging theorem premises,
or validating a candidate encoding of a fixed closed Lean object is not an exact decision of that
Lean claim.  A checker for a fixed closed object can be exact only when a checked canonical decoder
or equivalent correspondence evidence ties the runtime encoding to that specific Lean object;
without that bridge it remains a witness_validator.

Thus the order-13 checkers qualify because they independently decide ConvHolds and W1-W6 over the
decoded finite model.  The order-9 global invariant checker only evaluates opaque sentence and pack
parameters, while the frozen-independence and named-model checkers validate supplied candidates
without establishing that those candidates are the fixed Lean implementations named by the closed
theorems.  Those three therefore remain witness validators.
